  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nClear skies have been occurring this afternoon as high pressure \ncentered over the central CONUS dominates the pattern across \nthe Midwest. A strong pressure gradient is located over the CWA \ntoday, resulting from the aforementioned high pressure in the \nmid Mississippi River Valley and an area of low pressure \nundergoing cyclogenesis in the lee of the Canadian Rockies in \nnorthern Alberta and Saskatchewan. This strong pressure gradient\nhas been causing breezy southwest winds, which are forecast to \ncontinue through much of Sunday. These strong winds are leading \nto elevated fire weather concerns today. One thing of note is \nthat mixing will be somewhat limited this afternoon as a strong \nwarm nose at 850mb occurs, keeping min RH in the upper 20% to \nlow 30% range for most locations. \n\nThe WAA resulting from the strong southwest flow will cause low \ntemps tonight to be significant warmer compared to last night, in \nsome portions of the CWA as much as 10-15 degrees warmer. Cloud \ncover is expected to increase tonight as a cold front associated \nwith the developing low pressure in Canada arrives to the Upper \nMidwest. Precipitation is not expected with this cold frontal \npassage on Sunday. In addition to an increase in cloud cover, this \ncold frontal passage is forecast to result in easing winds on Sunday \nas high pressure builds into the CWA behind the front. \n\nThis pattern of high pressure is anticipated to be the dominant \npattern for much of this upcoming week, with very little to no \nchances for precipitation for the next several days as an omega \nblock likely develops. Temperatures will be seeing a gradual warming \ntrend early this week, with highs increasing from the upper 50s to \nlow 60s on Monday to the low to mid 60s for most by Wednesday. \nGlobal models are generally in agreement that a shortwave trough \nwill ride over the omega block through northern Ontario on \nWednesday, which could bring increased cloud cover to the CWA. \nHowever, precipitation is currently forecast to remain north of the \nborder. \n\nThe question of when our next best chance for precipitation will be \nis highly uncertain. Global models and ensembles suggest the \npotential for this omega block to break down on Friday into next \nweekend, with a cutoff low over southern California and the Great \nBasin propagating northeast towards the Midwest as a shortwave moves \nthrough the Pacific Northwest and Northern Rockies. However, \nconfidence in this timing remains very low given how far out in the \nforecast period this is and the tendency for omega blocks to be slow \nto break down.",
